---
import ProjectLayout from "../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
import { projects } from "../data/projects";
import ProjectSection from "../components/ProjectSection.astro";
import PcbViewer from "../components/PcbViewer.astro";
import HardwareDemo from "../components/HardwareDemo.astro";
import VideoFacade from "../components/VideoFacade.astro";
import ResponsiveImage from "../components/ResponsiveImage.astro";
import "../styles/demo-impedance.css";
---
<ProjectLayout project={projects.impedance}>
      <ProjectSection id="overview" address="0x1010" title="Overview">
        <p>The CMU Hacker Fab develops low-cost, open-source alternatives to democratize nanofabrication and replace expensive semiconductor manufacturing equipment. One of its core tools is a DIY RF sputtering chamber, where plasma conditions make the chamber impedance shift throughout a deposition run.</p>
        <p>Traditionally, an operator keeps tuning variable capacitors by hand to reduce reflected power, measured as VSWR (Voltage Standing Wave Ratio). Commercial automated matching networks typically cost $3,000&ndash;$8,000+, making them a poor fit for a student-run fab. This project built a custom automated RF matching network to remove that manual tuning step at a much lower cost.</p>
      </ProjectSection>
      <ProjectSection id="system-overview" address="0x1020" title="System Overview">
        <p>The matcher replaces manual knob-turning with high-precision digital control. A Teensy 4.1 and custom T-network read forward and reverse power signals from the RF line, then drive 0.9&deg; stepper motors at 1/64 microstepping connected to air-variable capacitors.</p>
        <p>A closed-loop controller searches capacitor positions in real time so the chamber stays matched as plasma conditions drift, without an operator on the knobs.</p>
        <dl class="spec-list">
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Actuation</dt><dd>0.9&deg; NEMA 17 steppers at 1/64 microstepping</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Network</dt><dd>T-network with 500&nbsp;V air-variable caps and a custom ~2&nbsp;&micro;H coil (6 turns, 16&nbsp;AWG)</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Match speed</dt><dd>~5&nbsp;s from cold startup to lock</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Sensing noise</dt><dd>&plusmn;0.001 VSWR after buffering and supersampling</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <VideoFacade posterJpg="/assets/projects/impedance/thumbnail.jpg" posterWebp="/assets/projects/impedance/thumbnail.webp" width={1280} height={720} youtubeId="EvusrXpzN2c" playLabel="Play closed-loop VSWR tuning demo" title="Closed-loop tuning" caption="Video: automated matching converges toward the target VSWR." />
      </ProjectSection>
      <ProjectSection id="pcb-design" address="0x1030" title="PCB Design">
        <p>I designed a custom two-layer PCB around the Teensy 4.1, motor drivers, and active cooling. The board needed careful shielding and grounding to suppress RF interference in the feedback loop, keeping the microcontroller and drivers reliable in a high-noise environment.</p>
        <PcbViewer schematic="/assets/projects/impedance/kicad/impedance_matcher.kicad_sch" pcb="/assets/projects/impedance/kicad/impedance_matcher.kicad_pcb" layerPreset="front-with-back" sourceUrl="https://github.com/hacker-fab/impedance-matcher/tree/main/hardware/PCB" />
        <figure class="visual pcb-figure" data-plate="Control board layout">
          <ResponsiveImage asset="impedance-pcb" alt="RF matcher control board PCB layout with Teensy 4.1, stepper motor drivers, and VSWR sense header" />
        
          <figcaption class="sr-only">Control board layout</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </ProjectSection>
      <ProjectSection id="sensor-integration" address="0x1040" title="Sensor Integration">
        <p>To measure VSWR, I reverse-engineered a COTS Surecom SW-112 VSWR meter and tapped into its internal toroidal sensing lines, then built an analog sensing path into the Teensy&rsquo;s ADCs around it.</p>
        <dl class="spec-list">
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Source</dt><dd>Reverse-engineered Surecom SW-112 toroidal sense lines</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Buffering</dt><dd>TLV2372IP op-amps isolate the Teensy ADC inputs</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Line impedance</dt><dd>~1 M&Omega; high-impedance path, preserved to avoid loading the sense lines</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Why it matters</dt><dd>Prevents voltage sag that would otherwise alter RF behavior mid-measurement</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </ProjectSection>
      <ProjectSection id="firmware" address="0x1050" title="Firmware">
        <p>The firmware processes digitized forward and reverse power, runs the coordinate descent matcher, and drives the actuators in real time. I also built a local interface with an I2C OLED and rotary encoder so operators can switch between automated and manual tuning while monitoring VSWR and power telemetry.</p>
        <div class="impedance-demo-row">
          <HardwareDemo demo="impedance-matcher" className="hardware-demo--impedance" fallbackSrc="/assets/projects/impedance/deployed.webp" fallbackAlt="Deployed impedance matcher showing VSWR on the OLED" fallbackWidth={1200} fallbackHeight={900} fallbackCaption="Interactive OLED demo unavailable. Showing deployed hardware." frameLabel="Interactive 128 by 64 OLED UI demo" captionClass="demo-instructions"><strong>Explore the hardware interface</strong><span class="demo-input-row"><b>Pointer</b><span>Click OLED buttons. Wheel or trackpad through Settings.</span></span><span class="demo-input-row"><b>Touch</b><span>Tap buttons. Swipe vertically through Settings and horizontally to adjust values.</span></span><span class="demo-input-row"><b>Keyboard</b><span>Use arrows to navigate and Enter to select.</span></span></HardwareDemo>
        </div>
      </ProjectSection>
      <ProjectSection id="control-algorithm" address="0x1060" title="Control Algorithm">
        <p>Each iteration, the firmware probes one motor axis at a time: it takes a small step, measures the change in cost, and uses the ratio as an approximate gradient to scale the next step.</p>
        <div class="eq-stack">
          <div class="eq-block">
            <span class="eq-block-label">Measurement / cost</span>
            <div class="eq-line" role="math" aria-label="VSWR equals mean forward plus mean reverse over mean forward minus mean reverse">
              <span class="eq-sym">VSWR</span>
              <span class="eq-op">=</span>
              <span class="eq-frac">
                <span class="eq-num"><span class="eq-bar">V</span><sub>fwd</sub> + <span class="eq-bar">V</span><sub>rev</sub></span>
                <span class="eq-den"><span class="eq-bar">V</span><sub>fwd</sub> &minus; <span class="eq-bar">V</span><sub>rev</sub></span>
              </span>
            </div>
            <div class="eq-line" role="math" aria-label="J equals VSWR minus 1, squared">
              <span class="eq-sym">J</span>
              <span class="eq-op">=</span>
              <span>(VSWR &minus; 1)<sup>2</sup></span>
            </div>
            <span class="eq-note"><span class="eq-bar">V</span> is the mean of N = 300 ADC samples, supersampled to suppress 13.56&nbsp;MHz switching noise.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="eq-block">
            <span class="eq-block-label">Finite-difference step</span>
            <div class="eq-line" role="math" aria-label="g-tilde equals delta J over delta theta, approximately negative gradient of J">
              <span class="eq-sym eq-tilde">g</span>
              <span class="eq-op">=</span>
              <span class="eq-frac">
                <span class="eq-num">&Delta;J</span>
                <span class="eq-den">&Delta;&theta;</span>
              </span>
              <span class="eq-op">&asymp;</span>
              <span>&minus;&nabla;J</span>
            </div>
            <div class="eq-line" role="math" aria-label="delta theta command equals alpha times g-tilde, alpha equals 0.025">
              <span class="eq-sym">&Delta;&theta;<sub>cmd</sub></span>
              <span class="eq-op">=</span>
              <span>&alpha; &middot; <span class="eq-tilde">g</span></span>
              <span class="eq-aside">(&alpha; = 0.025)</span>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="eq-block">
            <span class="eq-block-label">Step clamp</span>
            <div class="eq-line" role="math" aria-label="delta theta is in the closed interval from pi over 700 to pi over 36">
              <span class="eq-sym">&Delta;&theta;</span>
              <span class="eq-op">&isin;</span>
              <span class="eq-bracket">[</span>
              <span class="eq-frac">
                <span class="eq-num">&pi;</span>
                <span class="eq-den">700</span>
              </span>
              <span class="eq-op">,</span>
              <span class="eq-frac">
                <span class="eq-num">&pi;</span>
                <span class="eq-den">36</span>
              </span>
              <span class="eq-bracket">]</span>
            </div>
            <span class="eq-note">Lower bound escapes the noise floor; upper bound keeps the small-step approximation valid.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <HardwareDemo demo="impedance-heatmap" className="hardware-demo--heatmap" fallbackSrc="/assets/projects/impedance/cover.webp" fallbackAlt="Automated impedance matcher hardware" fallbackWidth={1520} fallbackHeight={1140} fallbackCaption="Heatmap demo unavailable. Showing matcher hardware." frameLabel="VSWR convergence heatmap over the two capacitor angles, showing the coordinate-descent path"><strong>Coordinate descent</strong><span>The marker follows successive tuning steps toward the minimum VSWR.</span></HardwareDemo>
      </ProjectSection>
      <ProjectSection id="results" address="0x1070" title="Results">
        <p>During a deployed argon sputtering-chamber test, the matcher held lock until the plasma extinguished from pump brownout or debris, not matcher drift. The design is open-source for other Hacker Fabs to replicate.</p>
        <dl class="spec-list">
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Stabilized VSWR</dt><dd>&asymp;1.2 at ~95&ndash;100&nbsp;W forward during the deployed chamber test</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Chamber</dt><dd>5.40&times;10<sup>&minus;3</sup>&nbsp;hPa Ar after strike</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Sustained run</dt><dd>~60&nbsp;min until plasma extinguished (pump/debris)</dd></div>
          <div class="spec-row"><dt>Build cost</dt><dd>$636.65 vs $3k&ndash;$8k commercial</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <figure class="visual visual--bare chart-figure" data-plate="VSWR over 60 min deposition">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 760 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="vswr-chart-title vswr-chart-desc">
            <title id="vswr-chart-title">VSWR over a 60-minute deposition run</title>
            <desc id="vswr-chart-desc">VSWR starts above 4 during cold match, settles near 1.2 within the first few minutes, holds that lock for about an hour, then spikes when the plasma extinguishes.</desc>
            <line class="chart-grid" x1="48" y1="16" x2="744" y2="16" />
            <line class="chart-grid" x1="48" y1="73" x2="744" y2="73" />
            <line class="chart-grid" x1="48" y1="130" x2="744" y2="130" />
            <line class="chart-grid" x1="48" y1="187" x2="744" y2="187" />
            <line class="chart-grid" x1="48" y1="244" x2="744" y2="244" />
            <rect class="chart-frame" x="48" y="16" width="696" height="228" />
            <text class="chart-axis" x="40" y="20" text-anchor="end">5</text>
            <text class="chart-axis" x="40" y="77" text-anchor="end">4</text>
            <text class="chart-axis" x="40" y="134" text-anchor="end">3</text>
            <text class="chart-axis" x="40" y="191" text-anchor="end">2</text>
            <text class="chart-axis" x="40" y="248" text-anchor="end">1</text>
            <text class="chart-label" x="48" y="268">0</text>
            <text class="chart-label" x="218" y="268" text-anchor="middle">15</text>
            <text class="chart-label" x="389" y="268" text-anchor="middle">30</text>
            <text class="chart-label" x="551" y="268" text-anchor="middle">45</text>
            <text class="chart-label" x="722" y="268" text-anchor="middle">60 min</text>
            <polyline class="chart-series" points="48.0,59.1 55.4,132.1 62.8,46.7 70.2,19.7 77.6,227.8 85.0,233.6 99.8,232.3 114.6,233.2 129.4,232.9 144.3,233.1 159.1,233.1 173.9,233.1 188.7,233.0 203.5,233.0 218.3,232.9 233.1,232.9 247.9,232.8 262.7,232.8 277.5,232.7 292.3,232.7 307.1,232.6 322.0,232.5 336.8,232.4 351.6,232.3 366.4,232.3 381.2,232.1 396.0,232.2 410.8,232.1 425.6,232.1 440.4,232.1 455.2,232.0 470.0,232.0 484.9,231.9 499.7,231.8 514.5,231.8 529.3,231.7 544.1,231.7 558.9,231.7 573.7,231.6 588.5,231.6 603.3,231.6 618.1,231.5 632.9,231.5 647.7,231.5 662.6,231.3 677.4,231.3 692.2,231.3 707.0,231.3 721.8,231.2 729.2,232.9 736.6,232.2 744.0,90.2" />
            <text class="chart-annot" x="100" y="218">match lock</text>
          </svg>
        
          <figcaption class="sr-only">VSWR over 60 min deposition</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="visual" data-plate="Deployed matcher">
          <ResponsiveImage asset="impedance-deployed" alt="Automated impedance matcher prototype in the Hacker Fab showing VSWR 1.235 on the OLED display" />
        
          <figcaption class="sr-only">Deployed matcher</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </ProjectSection>

  <script is:inline type="module" src="/js/kicanvas-config.js" slot="scripts"></script>
</ProjectLayout>
